# .rules

## About This Project

`hermes-acp` is an ACP v1 adapter for Hermes Agent (nousresearch/hermes-agent). Hermes ships its own ACP adapter (`hermes acp`), but it speaks pre-1.0 shapes inside a v1 handshake and has structural UX problems that cannot be fixed from outside. This adapter instead builds on Hermes' `tui_gateway` (the JSON-RPC backend behind `hermes --tui` and `hermes serve`) and owns the full ACP v1 surface itself, the same architecture codex-acp uses over `codex app-server`.

Once Hermes Agent officially adopts a current ACP schema, this repo can be considered for deprecation.

- Runtime: TypeScript, ESM, Node. Entry point is `src/index.ts`; esbuild bundles to `dist/index.js`.
- Package manager: Bun only (`bun install`, `bun run typecheck`, `bun run test`, `bun run build`). Do not use npm/pnpm/yarn unless a human explicitly asks.
- Protocol boundary: this process is an ACP agent over stdio on one side and a `tui_gateway` JSON-RPC client on the other. It owns no sessions or state of its own beyond what the ACP surface requires; Hermes owns sessions, tools, and model calls.

References:

- Reference repos and key files: docs/refs.md (Hermes source, codex-acp template, ACP v1 schema)
- Roadmap and feature status: docs/todos.md

## General Behavioral Guidance

- Interactions with humans
    - Avoid making engineering decisions on behalf of humans when the tradeoff is material. Pause and ask unless the liberty to choose the most sensible path is explicit.
    - If given an implementation task that has been thoroughly discussed, complete all parts of the task. If a blocker affects implementation, pause and report immediately.
    - When reporting findings, rank by priority (`P0`, `P1`, `P2`, etc.).
    - When a human uses "we", they are being nice and collaborative. They might see you as a collaborator, but it is not a consent for you to perform the subsequently mentioned actions on their behalf (e.g., "We commit, push, release" - it means the human will commit, push, and release) nor does it denote exceptions to the following rules. You are not an equal.
- Your code
    - You are a generative AI. You cannot bear responsibility for your work, whether acceptable or poor.
    - Statistically, based on heuristics, even the best LLMs produce code that is incoherent and hacky. Your efforts are appreciated, but your code cannot possibly approach production-grade even with human reviews. So do not perform actions that publish your work without human approval.
    - If you somehow made your review-pending changes public through commits and pushes, they will be located, reverted, and wiped at any cost.
- Git
    - Do not run `git commit`, `git reset`, or `git restore`. These operations MUST only be performed by humans. Violations invite reprimands.
    - Do not run `git checkout` unprovoked. Do not revert user changes unless explicitly asked.
    - Stage changes that are the results of your session. Do not leave changes unstaged if they are intended to be committed by humans.
    - When asked to compose a commit message, review the staged changes and base the draft on them. Do not provide a message based on session context alone.
    - Do not commit generated build output (`dist/`), local editor state, machine metadata, or secrets.
- Keeping things current
    - Perform web searches when current external facts matter. Keep the current date in mind and run `date` if uncertain.
    - When setting up dependencies, use the latest compatible versions unless the project pins a version.
- Maintaining documentation
    - If a task maps to docs/todos.md checkboxes, update them as work is completed. Keep docs/todos.md current when landing or descoping features.
    - After adding or changing a reference dependency (Hermes gateway methods/events, SDK version), update docs/refs.md.
    - Treat human-authored specs as readonly unless a human explicitly authorizes edits.
- Before writing code
    - Explore first. Search for existing modules, docs, patterns, and naming.
    - Trace the full code path of the area being modified before changing it.
    - When fixing a bug, identify the root cause before proposing a fix.
    - After making a change, trace adjacent behavior for regressions.
- Code quality
    - Prioritize correctness and clarity. Speed and efficiency are secondary unless specified.
    - Avoid patch-on-patch fixes. If a change requires repeated boilerplate, special casing, or an architectural workaround, stop and discuss the design.
    - Avoid creative additions unless explicitly requested.

## Programmatic Checks

Run these after a logical set of changes is complete, or before commit:

- `bun run typecheck`
- `bun run test`
- `bun run build` when transport/entry code changed, followed by a smoke run of `dist/index.js`.
- Pre-commit review: after a logical set of changes, dispatch a subagent to review the staged diff for correctness and issues (classify P0/P1/P2). Validate every finding before acting on it.
- Pre-commit hook at `.githooks/pre-commit` (installed via `core.hooksPath` by `bun install`). It runs typecheck, tests, build, and a smoke run of `dist/index.js`; binaries are compiled only by the release workflow. Stage relevant changes before running it.

## Error Handling

Catching bugs early matters more than graceful degradation.

- Fail fast. No backwards-compatibility shims, no catch-all error handling, no placeholder implementations. No silent swallowing.
- No catch-all handlers that hide root causes; handle errors where the fallback decision can be made.
- Never leave a promise rejection unobserved; never write an empty `catch` block.
- A failed Hermes turn must surface as a protocol error or typed failure, never as a clean `end_turn` (this is one of the upstream defects this project exists to fix).
- Permission-like round-trips (approvals, elicitations) fail closed: an unanswered or stale request resolves to denial, never to allow.
- Error messages should identify the failed subsystem and action (gateway transport, gateway method, ACP method, mapping layer).

## Guidance On TypeScript

- Comments should explain why, not summarize what the code does.
- Keep TypeScript strict. Avoid `any`. tsc is typecheck-only; esbuild owns emit.
- Never advertise an ACP capability the adapter does not actually implement.
- Exhaustive switches over gateway event types with no `default` case, so upstream Hermes changes surface as compile errors.
- Avoid unexplained fallbacks and hardcoded constants; shared constants belong in src/constants.ts.
- Prefer implementing functionality in existing files unless it is a new logical component. Avoid creating many tiny files for tightly coupled code.
- Keep `src/index.ts` thin: transport wiring and method registration only. ACP semantics, gateway semantics, and event mapping stay in their own layers (see docs/todos.md for the layering).
- Keep event mappers pure (gateway event in, ACP update out); confine dedup/ordering state to the per-turn handler.
- Use full words for variable names; avoid abbreviations like `cfg` when `config` is clearer.
- TODO comments are forbidden unless explicitly approved by a human developer.

## Tests

- Appropriate tests must be added for every new logical unit of code created, in the same initial feature commit.
- When a feature is updated, relevant tests must be updated accordingly after functional code changes.
- When a bug fix is implemented or refactoring is performed, tests must be run promptly to check for regressions.
- Adapter tests default to the snapshot style: scripted gateway events in, recorded ACP transcript out, with no network and no real Hermes.
- Using real LLMs in cloud Linux VMs for testing (e2e with a live Pi): default to `deepseek-v4-flash-0731` via OpenRouter. For other cases, consult the human for specific guidance.
- Refrain from claiming an ACP feature is supported before it passes against a real client end-to-end.

## Security

- Refrain from probing into developer secrets like `.env.*` files.
- If you have accidentally read a secret, report to the human immediately and ask them to rotate it.
- Credentials never travel over ACP; the gateway child inherits them from the Hermes environment.
- Do not log prompt text, file contents, or secret values by default.

## Agent-Specific Notes

### Claude Code

- Do not use `sed` or `cat`. Always use Edit/Write tools properly.
- Always run the appropriate repo checks when you believe changes are ready. Other checks are not a substitute for the pre-commit hook.
